{
	"name": "Vagabond",
	"type": "background",
	"img": "icons/environment/wilderness/terrain-river-road-gray.webp",
	"system": {
		"description": "<p><strong>Ability Score Increase:</strong> Strength or Dexterity +1, any other attribute +1.</p><p><strong>Skill Proficiencies:</strong> Animal Handling, Deception, expertise in Deception to appear nonthreatening</p><p><strong>Tool Proficiencies:</strong> Tinker Tools (Used for general repairs checks on houses, farms, and the like)</p><p><strong>Suggested Equipment (Cost 4 gold 7 silver):</strong> Crowbar, hammer, mess tin, hemp rope 50ft, tinderbox.</p><p><em><strong>Feature:</strong></em><strong> A Day’s Labor.</strong> Honest labor hardly satisfies most adventurers, who prefer to use their influence or persuasion to gain themselves a night in an inn or an hour on a stage. But for the classic vagabond, holding your hat in your hand while asking honest folk if they have work that needs doing is the surest, and most honest, way to a hot meal and a safe place to rest your head. In exchange for 1 hour of labor doing repairs and tending animals you can secure a moderate lifestyle of good food and sleeping in sheltered haylofts away from the troubles and vices of a tavern or other place of ill repute.</p><p><em><strong>Adventures and Advancement</strong></em>: While the call for skilled farmhands is common, it’s rarely going to garner the attention of the wealthy or renowned. However, your reputation as an adventurer will, soon enough, have people looking for you; either to employ you in a new venture or to seek payback for an old one. If you remain in town for a full week, your reputation catches up with you, and the settlement you’re in becomes your new ‘Prestige Center’ as word of who you are spreads.</p><p><strong>Connections and Memento:</strong> Roll 1d10, choose, or make up your own.</p><p>@Embed[Compendium.a5e.a5e-roll-tables.RollTable.AHI0TXPahueY3NFd caption=false cite=true rollable=true]</p><p>@Embed[Compendium.a5e.a5e-roll-tables.RollTable.CpZHu9by5xyFa0xS caption=false cite=true rollable=true]</p>",
